Correct
A cloud flow calls an external HTTP endpoint that occasionally returns 429 and 502 responses. The team wants the action to recover automatically from temporary failures without adding a separate error branch for every call.



Which control should you configure?



Select only one answer.

A. Scope container

Explanation
The Scope container is used to group actions together and define the scope of the actions within it. While it can help organize actions, it does not specifically address handling temporary failures or retries in case of 429 or 502 responses from an external HTTP endpoint.
Your answer is correct
B. Exponential retry policy

Explanation
The Exponential retry policy is the correct choice in this scenario. It allows the action to automatically retry the HTTP call with increasing time intervals between retries in case of temporary failures like 429 and 502 responses. This helps in recovering from transient errors without the need to create separate error branches for each call.
C. Failed terminate action

Explanation
The Failed terminate action is used to define what action should be taken when an action fails. While it is important for error handling, it does not provide the functionality of automatically retrying failed actions in case of temporary failures like 429 and 502 responses from an external HTTP endpoint.
D. Configure run after

Explanation
Configure run after is used to define the conditions under which an action should run based on the outcome of previous actions. While it can help control the flow of actions, it does not specifically address the need for automatic retries in case of temporary failures like 429 and 502 responses.
Overall explanation
An exponential retry policy is the best fit for transient HTTP failures because Power Automate lets you configure retry policy at the action level, and Microsoft recommends exponential intervals for temporary network or service problems. That directly addresses intermittent 429 and 5xx-style failures without forcing you to build custom branching around every single connector call.



This is also the most targeted control for the requirement. Configure run after handles what happens after an action ends with a particular status, but it does not itself retry the failed action. Scopes help organize logic, and Terminate stops the run, but neither is the primary recovery mechanism for transient faults.



Why the other options are incorrect:

• A. A scope container is useful for grouping related actions and for building try/catch-style patterns. However, it doesn’t retry a flaky HTTP request by itself. You would still need another control to handle temporary connector failures. That makes it a good organizational tool, but not the best answer for automatic transient recovery.

• C. A failed terminate action is appropriate when you want to stop the run and return a failed status. That is the opposite of the stated requirement, which is to recover automatically from temporary failures when possible. Using Terminate here would end the flow instead of attempting a retry. It is a useful error-escalation control, but not a transient-fault recovery control.

• D. Configure run after determines whether a later action runs after a prior action succeeds, fails, times out, or is skipped. It is essential for branching and catch/finally patterns, but it does not resend the failed request. In this scenario, the team wants the action itself to retry automatically. That is why retry policy is the stronger fit.

